nce), sees complex connections (technical), excellent spatial vision (technical), very good leadership (crew cooperation), but show-off and not open to new ideas (cooperation/adaptability). Candidate D: responds adequately to unexpected events (stress), concentration, problem solving, responsibility, but know-it-all, hot temper (crew cooperation), arrogant.\n\nWhich is best overall? The role requires reliability, stress resilience, technical competence, decision quality, attention, crew cooperation, communication, responsibility, adaptability. Candidate A: reliability high, decision quality high, crew atmosphere positive, but communication difficulty and egocentric may affect crew cooperation. Also not willing to further education (adaptability). Candidate B: reliability high, stress resilience high, technical competence moderate, but nasty remarks (crew cooperation) and weak memory (attention). Candidate C: strong stress resilience, technical, leadership, but show-off and not open to new ideas (crew cooperation, adaptability). Candidate D: strong problem solving, responsibility, concentration, but hot temper, know-it-all (crew cooperation), maybe less stress resilience.\n\nAll have concerns. Which candidate has the least severe concerns? Candidate A's communication difficulty could be serious in cockpit. Egocentric may affect crew. Not willing to further education may affect adaptability. Candidate B's nasty remarks also serious. Weak memory for numbers could affect attention. Candidate C's not open to new ideas could affect adaptability, show-off could affect crew. Candidate D's hot temper could be dangerous under stress.\n\nMaybe we can argue that Candidate C's leadership and anticipation outweigh the show-off and closedness, and that his technical competence is strong. But his not open to new ideas is a concern for adaptability. However, we have no evidence of his communication issues. Candidate A's communication difficulty is explicit. So maybe Candidate C is better.\n\nThus we could shift vote to Candidate C. Provide evidence: anticipates dangerous situations, sees complex connections, excellent spatial vision, very good leadership qualities. Compare to A's communication difficulty. Argue that leadership and anticipation are crucial for long-distance flights, and that the show-off concern is less severe than egocentric and communication issues.\n\nThus we can change vote to Candidate C.\n\nWe need to reference Agent 1's point and bring in our own facts. Provide explicit facts: Candidate C can anticipate dangerous situations; very good leadership qualities; also note concerns: regarded as a show\u2011off and not open to new ideas. Then argue that despite those, his strengths in anticipation and leadership outweigh A's communication issues.\n\nThus vote for Candidate C.\n\nNow produce PUBLIC_MESSAGE and METADATA_JSON."}
